<LegalPage title="Privacy Policy" updated="October 26, 2026">
  <p>
    This Privacy Policy explains how BottleCount ("the app", "we", "us") uses
    your data when you use the BottleCount web application.
  </p>

  <h2>1. What BottleCount does</h2>
  <p>
    BottleCount helps you plan parties and manage tickets. It comes in three
    forms, and they differ in exactly one way that matters here: whether we hold
    any of your data at all.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Browser (free)</strong>: no account, no sign-in, and no server.
      Everything you enter stays in your browser.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Hosted</strong>: you sign in with Google, and we store an account
      record for you — and, if you share a party, that party.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Self-hosted</strong>: you run the server. Nothing described below
      reaches us — your deployment's operator is the data controller, and that
      is you.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Data stored in your browser</h2>
  <p>
    On every tier, your parties, drink menus, custom ingredients, cocktails,
    generated tickets, theme preference and app settings are stored locally in
    your browser (IndexedDB and local storage). On the free tier this is the
    only place any of it exists: we cannot read it, recover it, or hand it to
    anyone, and clearing your browser data deletes it permanently.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Data we hold when you sign in</h2>
  <p>Signing in with Google gives us the following, and nothing else:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Your Google account identifier, email address, display name and avatar
      URL, taken from the OpenID <em>openid</em>,
      <em>email</em> and <em>profile</em> scopes.
    </li>
    <li>
      Which plan your account is on, and the licence code you redeemed to get
      there.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We request no access to your Google Drive, Sheets, contacts, calendar or any
    other Google service. This record is stored in Cloudflare D1.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your session is a signed token in an <code>httpOnly</code> cookie named
    <code>session_token</code>, valid for seven days. It is a strictly necessary
    cookie: it exists only to keep you signed in, and the app sets no analytics
    or advertising cookies.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Parties you share, and data about your guests</h2>
  <p>
    A party stays in your browser until you share it. Sharing — which both the
    invite link and co-organisers need — stores the whole plan on our servers:
    its name, date and venue, drink menu, quantities, costs, budget, ticket
    price and any ways to be paid you add. That is what lets your co-organisers
    open and edit it. Everyone on a party sees the plan and the other
    organisers' names, email addresses and avatars; you can remove a
    co-organiser at any time.
  </p>
  <p>
    People holding the invite link see far less: only what an invitation shows —
    the party's name, date, venue (with its place on the map, if you picked
    one), cover image, and the description and practical info you write for them
    — and, once they say they are coming, the ways to be paid you added (a
    PayPal.me username, a Satispay or Revolut link, or a bank account holder and
    IBAN). Your menu, shopping list, costs and budget are never shown to them.
  </p>
  <p>
    A banner photo or poster you add to a shared party is stored on our servers
    (Cloudflare R2) and shown to your co-organisers, and to people holding the
    invite link while it is open. The banner is also shown on your guests'
    tickets, to whoever holds the ticket link. Before it leaves your device,
    your browser shrinks it and saves it again as a new image, which drops the
    metadata cameras embed, such as where and when a photo was taken. It is
    deleted when you remove or replace it, and when the party is deleted. Only
    upload images you have the right to share, and keep in mind that anyone with
    the link can see them.
  </p>
  <p>
    We then store one record per person who opens that link: the name they type,
    their answer, when they opened it and answered, and who forwarded it to
    them. Guests are not asked to create an account.
  </p>
  <p>
    If guests pay for your party (you set a ticket price and a way to be paid),
    a guest who says they are coming also gives an email address or a phone
    number, at least one. These are used only to send that guest their ticket
    for this party, by email or WhatsApp, from the organiser's own phone. You
    and your co-organisers can see them; nobody holding the link can, including
    the guest who entered them. A guest who changes their answer to no has them
    deleted. You can also add an email and phone number to a guest you type in
    yourself. When you mark a guest as paid we record when; BottleCount never
    sees or handles the payment itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    When you send a guest their ticket from a shared party, the ticket — their
    name and ticket code, signed with the party's key — is stored with their
    guest record, so the short link you send can open it. The link works only
    while the guest is coming (and, on a party guests pay for, marked as paid),
    and the ticket is deleted with the party.
  </p>
  <p>
    As the host, you decide what happens to those records. Closing the invite
    link stops new answers but keeps your guest list, so you can still send
    tickets and run the door. Stopping sharing the party deletes it from our
    servers together with every guest record, email addresses and phone numbers
    included. Under the GDPR you are the controller for your guests' data and we
    are your processor, which in plain terms means it is your party and your
    guest list — we hold it for you and delete it when you say so.
  </p>

  <h2>5. How we use it</h2>
  <p>
    Only to sign you in, to tell the app which features your plan includes, to
    run the features you use — keeping a shared party in step between its
    organisers, the invite link, and sending your guests their tickets — and to
    answer you if you contact support. We do not use your data for advertising
    or profiling, and we do not sell it or share it with third parties.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Processors</h2>
  <p>
    Cloudflare, Inc. hosts the application and the database. Google LLC provides
    sign-in. Both act as processors on our behalf; neither receives your data
    for their own purposes from us.
  </p>
  <p>
    Finding a venue on the map is the one place your browser talks to someone
    else directly, and only while you use it. What you type into the map search
    goes to Photon, komoot GmbH's place search, and the map itself is loaded
    from the OpenStreetMap Foundation's tile servers; both see your IP address
    and the request, as any website you open does. Typing the venue in by hand
    contacts neither. Your guests' invite page loads nothing from either: its
    "Open in Maps" link opens Google Maps only when a guest taps it.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Retention and deletion</h2>
  <p>
    We keep your account record for as long as your account exists. Email us to
    have it deleted and we will remove it, along with any licence association
    and every party you own, within 30 days. A shared party is kept until you
    stop sharing it, which deletes it from our servers together with its guest
    records; closing only the invite link keeps them, so you can still send
    tickets and run the door. Data held only in your browser is deleted by
    clearing your browser's storage for this site, which happens without
    involving us.
  </p>

  <h2>8. Your rights and revoking access</h2>
  <p>
    If you are in the EU or the UK, you have the right to access, correct,
    export, restrict, object to and erase your personal data, and to complain to
    your local data protection authority. Email us to exercise any of them.
  </p>
  <p>You can revoke BottleCount's access to your Google account at any time:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Go to
      <a
        href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noreferrer"
      >
        myaccount.google.com/permissions
      </a>.
    </li>
    <li>Find BottleCount in the list of third‑party apps.</li>
    <li>Click "Remove access".</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    BottleCount's use and transfer of information received from Google APIs
    adheres to the
    <a
      href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noreferrer"
    >
      Google API Services User Data Policy
    </a>, including the Limited Use requirements.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Children's privacy</h2>
  <p>
    BottleCount is not directed to children under 13. If you are under 13,
    please do not use this app without parental supervision and consent.
  </p>

  <h2>10. Changes</h2>
  <p>
    We may update this Privacy Policy from time to time. When we do, we will
    update the "Last updated" date at the top of this page.
  </p>

  <h2>11. Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions? Contact the developer at
    <a href="mailto:marcoingalera@gmail.com">marcoingalera@gmail.com</a>.
  </p>
</LegalPage>
